In [Discussion #259](https://github.com/pngwn/MDsveX/discussions/259), pngwn gave four reasons for leaving unified: performance, API instability, poor documentation, and possible changes to allowed syntax extensions. The new parser would have its own plugin system instead of remark and rehype plugins.

[Penguin-flavoured markdown](https://github.com/pngwn/MDsveX/discussions/293), or PFM, proposes syntax changes too. On [Reddit, as evilpingwin](https://www.reddit.com/r/sveltejs/comments/15rz6al/thoughts_on_mdsvex_moving_away_from_unified/), pngwn said GFM's features would remain supported, but the syntax would not always be identical. He promised an automated migration path.

## Where we stopped

Our local check of the published `mdsvex@1.0.0-next.0` stopped on this minimal CommonMark input:

```markdown
- **`x`** x
- y
```

The published migrator converted it to PFM:

```text
- *`x`* x
- y
```

The next.0 compiler printed `Infinite loop detected`. The second list item disappeared. The parser reported zero errors. A separate probe passed a rehype plugin to `compile`; the plugin was never called. These are observations about that published prerelease, not every later commit.

We kept 0.12.8. [The fence](/lore/the-fence) is not permission to rewrite valid prose around a parser defect.

The irony is in the [published migrator](https://registry.npmjs.org/@mdsvex/migrate/0.2.0-next.0): it depends on `unified`, `remark-parse`, and `remark-gfm`. The new renderer leaves that ecosystem; the migration into it still reads CommonMark with remark.
